\description{
Compute the one-dimensional site frequency spectrum (SFS) of each
population, i.e. the number of loci with 0, 1, 2, ... copies of the
alternate (or minor) allele. Missing genotypes are dealt with as in
\code{easySFS} (which relies on \code{dadi}), so that the spectra can be used for
demographic inference with \code{dadi}, \code{moments} or \code{fastsimcoal2}. See
\code{\link[=pairwise_pop_sfs]{pairwise_pop_sfs()}} for the joint spectra of pairs of populations, and
\code{\link[=nwise_pop_sfs]{nwise_pop_sfs()}} for the joint spectrum of all populations.
}
\details{
\strong{Missing data.} Because of missing genotypes, the number of allele
copies called at a locus varies from locus to locus. Each population is
therefore projected down to a fixed number of allele copies, \code{n_proj}
(Marth et al. 2004; Gutenkunst et al. 2009): a locus with \eqn{n} called
allele copies, \eqn{k} of which are alternate, contributes to cell
\eqn{j} of the spectrum the hypergeometric probability of drawing \eqn{j}
alternate copies when sampling \code{n_proj} of its \eqn{n} copies without
replacement. Loci with fewer than \code{n_proj} called copies are dropped. Each
locus is thus spread over several cells, and the spectrum contains
fractional numbers of sites. This is the approach of \code{easySFS} (with the
\code{-a} option, i.e. using all loci).

\strong{Choosing the projection.} Projecting to more allele copies gives more
resolution (more segregating sites, especially rare variants), but drops
more loci with missing data. Following the advice of the \code{dadi} manual and
\code{easySFS}, use \code{\link[=pop_sfs_preview]{pop_sfs_preview()}} to compute the number of segregating
sites for each possible projection, and choose a value that keeps most
segregating sites. If \code{n_proj} is not given, each population is projected
to the number of allele copies that maximises its number of segregating
sites (the \code{best} projection in \code{\link[=pop_sfs_preview]{pop_sfs_preview()}}).

\strong{Folding and polarisation.} Unless \code{folded = FALSE}, the spectrum is
folded (i.e. it counts copies of the minor allele), as the ancestral allele
is not usually known. An unfolded spectrum counts copies of the derived
allele. By default (\code{ancestral = NULL}), the reference allele is taken to
be ancestral, as in \code{easySFS}, so that the spectrum counts copies of the
alternate allele: this is only meaningful if the alleles of the
\code{gen_tibble} are polarised (note that some operations, such as merging
datasets with \code{\link[=rbind]{rbind()}}, can swap the reference and alternate alleles).
Otherwise, \code{ancestral} gives the ancestral allele of each locus (e.g.
from an outgroup, or the \code{AA} field of a vcf), either as a column of the
loci table or as a vector: loci whose ancestral allele is the alternate
allele are polarised (copies of the reference allele are counted), and
loci whose ancestral allele is missing (\code{NA}) or is neither of their two
alleles are dropped, as in \code{dadi}. Alleles are compared ignoring case.
Polarisation does not change a folded spectrum, so \code{ancestral} is ignored
(with a warning) when \code{folded = TRUE}.

\strong{Masked cells.} As in \code{dadi}, the corners of the spectrum (sites
monomorphic in the projected sample) are masked, and so are the cells that
were folded out in a folded spectrum. They are reported in the \code{masked}
column (or the \code{mask} attribute), but keep their values.

Each spectrum only uses the loci that have enough called allele copies in
its own populations. Diploid, polyploid, mixed-ploidy and pseudohaploid
data are supported: each individual contributes as many allele copies as
its ploidy (one for pseudohaploids). The spectrum is computed from the
genotypes currently in use: if the \code{gen_tibble} uses imputed genotypes (see
\code{\link[=gt_set_imputed]{gt_set_imputed()}}), these are treated as called genotypes, so you will
usually want to use \code{gt_set_imputed(x, FALSE)} first.

Note that \code{easySFS} samples a single SNP per \code{CHROM} (i.e. per RAD locus)
by default; to do the same, thin the loci (e.g. with \code{\link[=select_loci]{select_loci()}})
before computing the spectra.
}
